From 0aee8d38129f217872d3b5f2c8d37acd67100b21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:20:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: HDR10+ sent as Dolby Vision, as an option

A display that takes Dolby Vision and not HDR10+
shows an HDR10+ video as HDR10: its dynamic metadata are lost. "HDR10+ as
Dolby Vision" (videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10plus, System > Display, off by
default) sends such a video as native Dolby Vision instead, with metadata
made from its own HDR10+ metadata, scene by scene
(dvbridge_hdr10plus_next()): the same HDR10 picture, with the peak and the
average of each scene that the studio's HDR10+ metadata measure. What
HDR10+ has and Dolby Vision is not given (the tone curve and the target
display, the saturation weight, the windows) is said in the help.

The demuxer looks at the first packets of a HEVC stream as it does for an
RPU without a record (ProbeDolbyVision()), and with the same rule that the
stream's own Dolby Vision always wins: an access unit with a picture, HDR10+
metadata (the T.35 SEI of ST 2094-40, read with CHevcSei) from which a
level 1 block can be made, no unit of an RPU or of an enhancement layer,
BT.2020 PQ, and the luminance of the mastering display (container or SEI)
make it Dolby Vision profile 8.1 from there on (CDVHdr10Plus), where the
setting is on, the user chose no other output for the video, the display
takes native Dolby Vision in a mode that carries it and is not sent
HDR10+ (DVBRIDGE::Hdr10PlusAsDolbyVision()). The stream says so
(doviFromHdr10Plus), and its decoder makes the metadata of each picture;
where the output is not native after all, its pictures are the HDR10+
pictures they were, never rebuilt as HDR10. A picture without HDR10+
metadata of its own counts as one without an RPU (CheckNativeDVPicture()):
a stretch of them goes to the ordinary output, fail closed.

What decides is DVBRIDGE::Hdr10PlusAsDolbyVisionFor() (DVPlaybackPolicy.h),
on what Hdr10PlusAsDolbyVision() reads: a display that is sent HDR10+ keeps
it, that is one that declares HDR10+ and whose driver sends the metadata of
each frame (HDR10PLUS_VSIF: CWinSystemGbm::GetDisplayHDRCapabilities()). A
host test drives it on an EDID with HDR10+ and one without, with and without
that property.

The Dolby Vision page of the player information says when the metadata
are made from HDR10+ (hades.dv.origin).
